# ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
# ║  CX LEXICON — the single token grammar for `cxparse` (the unified parser) ║
# ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
#
# PURPOSE. One scannable, unambiguous definition of every token the CX parser
# produces, plus the post-lexical scalar/array auto-typing rule. This file
# defines what the language *IS* going forward — the contract `cxparse` (the
# one parser that subsumes the current cx.parse + code.parse) is held to. It
# does NOT document what any current parser happens to do today.
#
# AUTHORITY. Each production cites the normative source it is extracted from:
#   [N]      → spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf production N (sibling file)
#   §3.x     → spec/03-approved/core/code.md §3 (lexical structure)
#   cxdm §y  → spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md
# For the TRANSCRIBED productions (tokens, §1-§8, §11) the cited source wins and
# THIS file is the bug if they disagree — the goal is zero drift; this is the
# consolidation. TWO sections DELIBERATELY DECIDE NEW behavior that SUPERSEDES
# the pre-v0.8.0 core spec (graduated 2026-06-03; core reconciled TO them):
#   • §9  BODY VALUE (@CHOICE-1): a no-comma body of self-delimiting tokens
#         (typed scalars / atoms / quoted strings — NO bareword) is a list of
#         DISCRETE auto-typed children (mixed content), NOT an array; a bareword
#         makes the whole no-comma body one prose string; a comma (or a `::T[]`/
#         `::[]` head) makes an array. REVISES the pre-v0.8.0 [25a] whitespace
#         auto-array — whitespace NEVER auto-arrays; the comma is the array signal.
#   • §10 ATTRIBUTES: scalar-only — DELIBERATELY REMOVES node-valued attributes
#         (formerly supported by grammar [55a] / ast.md / conversions.md).
# Those two are decisions of record here; core was reconciled TO them at
# graduation, not the reverse.
#
# SCOPE. Surface tokens + scalar/array auto-typing + collection literals and
# the `[` element-vs-array disambiguation (§11). This file is the SOLE home
# for all of that. The directive / clause-head / reserved-name vocabulary is
# large and stays in code.md §4.1 — referenced, not inlined. Higher
# composition (how elements/directives/programs nest) stays in grammar.ebnf.
#
# ANTI-DRIFT (one rule, one home). At G3 graduation (DONE 2026-06-03) these
# duplicated sources WERE gutted to pointers into this file — the rules MOVED
# here, not copied:
#   • grammar.ebnf [6]-[24] (lexical), [25]/[25a] (auto-type), [56]-[56g]
#     (collections) -> now "see lexicon.ebnf" pointers.
#   • code.md §3.1-§3.6 -> collapsed to a normative deferral.
# code.md itself does NOT go away: §4-§13 (evaluation, directives, errors)
# remain its sole domain.
#
# NOTATION.  ::= definition   |  alternation   ()  group   ?  0-or-1
#            *  0-or-more      +  1-or-more     [..] char class   '..' literal
#            #xNN unicode codepoint             /* … */  comment

# ─── 0. Source ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# §3.1  Source MUST be valid UTF-8. A leading BOM (EF BB BF) is consumed
#       silently; a BOM anywhere else → CXER0100.

# ─── 1. Whitespace & comments (skipped; never reach the parser) ────────────────
[L1]  S            ::= (#x20 | #x09 | #x0D | #x0A)+     /* §3.2 space tab CR LF */
[L2]  LineComment  ::= '#' [^#x0A]* (#x0A | EOF)        /* §3.3, [30b] */
[L3]  BlockComment ::= '[-' (Char* - (Char* ']' Char*)) ']'  /* §3.3, [30] */
#  NOTE  `[# … #]` is NOT a comment — it is RawText / CDATA [31]. Do not
#        confuse the two; this exact slip lived in the old lexer's header.

# ─── 2. Identifiers & names ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[L10]  Ident         ::= NameStartChar NameChar*       /* [6]/[6a] full Unicode */
[L10a] NameStartChar ::= [A-Z] | '_' | [a-z] | [#xC0-#xD6] | [#xD8-#xF6]
                       | [#xF8-#x2FF] | [#x370-#x37D] | [#x37F-#x1FFF]
                       | [#x200C-#x200D] | [#x2070-#x218F] | [#x2C00-#x2FEF]
                       | [#x3001-#xD7FF] | [#xF900-#xFDCF] | [#xFDF0-#xFFFD]
                       | [#x10000-#xEFFFF]             /* [6] MINUS ':' — see note */
[L10b] NameChar      ::= NameStartChar | '-' | '.' | [0-9] | #xB7
                       | [#x0300-#x036F] | [#x203F-#x2040]      /* [6a] */
#  FULL UNICODE name chars per grammar [6]/[6a] (XML-derived) — keeps CX⇄XML
#  bijective for non-ASCII element names. ASCII kebab-case `[a-z][a-z0-9-]*` is
#  the PREFERRED STYLE for new identifiers (§3.4), NOT a lexical restriction.
#  `_`-leading is VALID: `_private` is a legal Ident. `:` is NOT a NameChar —
#  `prefix:local` is FOLDED into a QName from its Ident `:` Ident parts ([L11]).
#  This is the ONE deliberate departure from grammar [7] (where `:` is a
#  NameChar). The two parser MODES realize the fold equivalently, NOT via one
#  physical tokenizer (the single-tokenizer flip was ruled architecturally
#  unsound — PLAN §4.3): PROGRAM mode lexes `:` as its own token; DATA mode is
#  scannerless and accepts `:` while scanning the name run (`cx.is_name_char`).
#  Same QName either way — equivalence pinned by `cxparse_namechar_fork_test`.
#  `.` is likewise mode-forked (the second and last name-char mode rule): in DATA
#  mode `.` IS a NameChar, so `a.b` reads as ONE dotted element name; in PROGRAM
#  mode `.` is NOT a name char — it is the path-step separator (`$u/..`) and the
#  decimal point (`0.5`, `[+ .5 .5]`). Both `.` and `:` are deliberate, pinned
#  data/program mode differences, not duplicated logic.
#  `--`/leading-`-`/trailing-`-` are not special; the number rule [L20] decides
#  whether a leading `-` is a sign.
[L11] QName      ::= Ident (':' Ident)?                /* [7a] prefix:local — parser-folded */
#  The SINGLE colon `:` folds into a QName (PROGRAM mode lexes it as its own
#  token; DATA mode scans it in-name, §4.3); QName/atom/type-guard/
#  map-entry are distinguished by the parser via context (§3.5, §3.6). `:` never
#  glues into an Ident. The FOUR (and only four) roles of a lone `:`:
#     prefix ':' local   namespace qualifier   `svg:rect`   ([7a])
#     ':' Ident          atom literal           `:ok`        ([122b], §3.6)
#     ':' TypeName        type-guard head        `:int`       (§5.4, pattern pos)
#     MapKey ':' value   map-entry separator    `{k: v}`     (§11 [L86])

# ─── 3. Number literals ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[L20] Number    ::= [+-]? (HexInt | Float | Integer)   /* [20] */
#  SIGN: a leading `+` or `-` immediately followed by a digit is part of the
#  Number token; a sign followed by a non-digit is a name char. The `+` is
#  ACCEPTED but redundant — canonical output drops it (`+8080` → `8080`). A `-`
#  *inside* a token (e.g. a date `2024-01-15`) is NOT a subtraction — see [L23].
#  The old program lexer shattered `2024-01-15` into `2024 -1 -15`; that is the
#  bug this rule forbids.
[L20a] HexInt   ::= '0x' HexDigit ('_'? HexDigit)*     /* [20a] checked before Integer */
[L20b] Float    ::= Mantissa ([eE] [+-]? Integer)?
                  | Integer [eE] [+-]? Integer         /* [20b] needs '.' or exponent */
[L20b1] Mantissa ::= Integer '.' Frac?                 /* 1.5, 1.  (trailing dot ok) */
                   | '.' Frac                          /* .5       (leading dot ok)  */
[L20b2] Frac     ::= [0-9] ('_'? [0-9])*
[L20c] Integer  ::= '0' | [1-9] ('_'? [0-9])*          /* [20c]/[20d] NO leading zero */
[L20d] HexDigit ::= [0-9a-fA-F]                         /* [11a] */
#  A leading `.` (`.5`) or trailing `.` (`1.`) IS a valid Float; canonical output
#  NORMALIZES to an explicit leading/trailing zero (`.5` → `0.5`, `1.` → `1.0`),
#  so each value has ONE spelling. Leading-zero INTEGER tokens like `02134` are
#  NOT Integer → they fall through to Text (preserves ZIP codes / SKUs); this rule
#  does NOT apply to the `0` in `0.5`. An integer literal that overflows i64
#  AUTO-PROMOTES to `bigint` (it stays numeric — magnitude never flips a number to
#  Text); explicit `[x::int <overflow>]` is a hard error. `_` between digits
#  is a readability aid, stripped before eval; it may not lead, trail, or double.

# ─── 4. Keyword / date / time scalar literals ────────────────────────────────────
[L21] BoolLiteral     ::= 'true' | 'false'             /* [21] */
[L22] NullLiteral     ::= 'null'                       /* [22] */
[L23] DateLiteral     ::= [0-9]{4} '-' [0-9]{2} '-' [0-9]{2}   /* [23] ONE token */
[L24] DateTimeLiteral ::= DateLiteral 'T' [0-9]{2} ':' [0-9]{2} ':' [0-9]{2}
                          ('.' [0-9]+)?
                          ('Z' | [+-] [0-9]{2} ':' [0-9]{2})?  /* [24] incl. Z / ±tz */
#  `2024-01-15`            → ONE date token       (NOT `2024 - 1 - 15`)
#  `2024-01-15T10:30:00Z`  → ONE datetime token   (the `Z`/offset is part of it)
#  CALENDAR VALIDITY: the shape rules above are necessary but NOT sufficient — a
#  Date/DateTime token must also be a real calendar value (month 1–12; day
#  1–days-in-month with Gregorian leap years; hour 0–23; minute/second 0–59;
#  zone offset in range). A shape-valid but calendar-invalid run (`2024-13-45`,
#  `2024-01-01T25:99:99`) is NOT a date/datetime token — it falls through to Text
#  (helper `cx.calendar_ok`). The extent recognizer is unchanged, so the token
#  stays ONE run (no fragmentation); only its classification differs.
[L27] DurationLiteral ::= [+-]? DurTerm+               /* exact span → ::duration */
[L27a] DurTerm        ::= Integer DurUnit
[L27b] DurUnit        ::= 'ns' | 'us' | 'ms' | 's' | 'm' | 'h' | 'd' | 'w'
[L28] PeriodLiteral   ::= [+-]? PerTerm+               /* calendar span → ::period */
[L28a] PerTerm        ::= Integer PerUnit
[L28b] PerUnit        ::= 'mo' | 'y'
#  DURATION is an EXACT span (fixed nanoseconds): ns, us, ms, s, m(inute), h,
#  d(=24h), w(=7d) — one or more terms, largest→smallest by convention (`500ms`,
#  `90m`, `1h30m`). It converts losslessly to i64 ns. PERIOD is a CALENDAR span —
#  mo(nth), y(ear) — whose length is NOT fixed (it needs an anchor date to
#  resolve), so it is a DISTINCT type, never an exact Duration. `m` is ALWAYS
#  minutes; months use `mo` (no collision). LEXING: recognized only when the
#  WHOLE token is integer+unit term(s) (else Text — `5mph` is Text); units match
#  LONGEST-first (`ms`/`mo` before `m`). A token mixing exact and calendar units
#  (`1y2h`) is NOT one literal (write a Duration and a Period separately). XML
#  image is ISO 8601 — Duration → `PT1H30M` / `P10D` / `P2W`; Period → `P1Y2M`.

# ─── 5. String literals (always Text; never auto-typed) ──────────────────────────
[L30] QuotedText   ::= "'" ( [^'\\] | EscapeSeq )* "'"            /* [10] single-quoted */
                     | '"' ( [^"\\] | EscapeSeq )* '"'            /* [10] double-quoted */
#  Single- and double-quoted strings are EQUIVALENT data string forms; the parser
#  dispatches on the opening quote and the same EscapeSeq set [L32] applies to
#  both. Canonical output prefers `'…'`, switching to `"…"` only when the content
#  contains a `'` (so it need not be escaped); a string containing both falls back
#  to a triple-quote or escaped form — see the renderer.
[L31] TripleQuoted ::= "'''" (Char* - (Char* "'''" Char*)) "'''"  /* [10b] raw, multiline */
#  TripleQuoted CONTENT is VERBATIM — NO escape processing (`\'` is two literal
#  bytes, not an escape) — but the common LEADING INDENT is STRIPPED (dedent).
#  DEDENT ALGORITHM (normative): the MINIMUM leading-whitespace run over all
#  NON-blank lines is removed from every line; a tab and a space EACH count as ONE
#  column (no tab expansion); blank / whitespace-only lines do NOT contribute to
#  the minimum but are re-emitted as-is; a line shorter than the minimum is fully
#  left-trimmed. The closing delimiter is found by MAXIMAL MUNCH: a run of 4+ `'`
#  ends the literal at the LAST three, so `''''…` embeds one literal `'`.
[L32] EscapeSeq    ::= '\\' ( "'" | '"' | '\\' | 'n' | 'r' | 't'
                            | 'u' HexDigit{4}
                            | 'U' HexDigit{8} )                  /* [11]/§3.7 */
#  The DECODED escape set is CLOSED and UNIFORM across single-, double-, and
#  (program) string literals: \\ \' \" \n \r \t \uXXXX \UXXXXXXXX. An UNKNOWN
#  backslash-sequence (`\` + any other char) is kept LITERALLY — both the
#  backslash and the char are preserved — so regex patterns like `'\d+'`, `'\w'`,
#  `'\s'` survive verbatim. This lenient pass-through is UNIFORM across the data
#  parser and the program lexer (no hard error). (TripleQuoted [L31] is verbatim:
#  no escape processing at all.) QuotedText / TripleQuoted are ALWAYS string Text
#  regardless of content: `'42'` is the string "42", never the int 42.
#  CODEPOINT VALIDITY: a `\u`/`\U` escape (and an XML `&#…;` char-ref) whose
#  decoded value is a UTF-16 SURROGATE (U+D800–U+DFFF) or is > U+10FFFF is
#  REJECTED — `cx-err:CXERLEX-CODEPOINT` — because the result must be a valid
#  Unicode scalar (the §0 valid-UTF-8 mandate + the CX⇄XML bijection forbid it).

# ─── 6. Atom literal ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[L40] Atom ::= ':' Ident                               /* [122b], §3.6 */
#  `:true` `:false` `:null` are FORBIDDEN in atom position → CXER0100
#  (they would lure readers into reading them as the bool/null). No other
#  names are reserved as atoms. Atoms render as `:NAME`, never `:"NAME"`.
#  An atom is an interned symbolic constant (≈ Clojure keyword / Erlang atom),
#  type-strict (`:ok ≠ "ok"`), NOT ordered (no `<`; cxdm §5.5), NOT a map key.
#  USE for match/case discriminants + small CLOSED tag sets (`:ok` `:prod`);
#  NOT for ordered enums (use int), unbounded/user values (use string), or
#  values needing a payload (use an element). See cxdm.md §2.3 (D4).

# ─── 7. Type annotation (glued; binds to the token on its LEFT) ───────────────────
[L50] TypeAnnotation ::= '::' ( TypeName ('[]')? | '[]' )         /* [26] */
[L51] TypeName       ::= 'int' | 'float' | 'bool' | 'string' | 'null'
                       | 'decimal' | 'bigint' | 'bytes' | 'atom'
                       | 'date' | 'datetime' | 'duration' | 'period' | 'instant'
                       | 'secret'
                       | 'i8' | 'i16' | 'i32' | 'i64'
                       | 'u8' | 'u16' | 'u32' | 'u64'
                       | 'f16' | 'f32' | 'f64'                    /* [26a]/[157a] */
#  Glued `::T` is valid in EVERY type position — element head (`port::u16`),
#  attribute (`port::u16=8080`), param, return, table column. The DOUBLE colon
#  is unambiguous; a single `:` is NEVER a type annotation — glued single `:`
#  is the NAMESPACE qualifier (`items:string` = element `string` in namespace
#  `items`, a QName [L11] — NOT a type). A parser that rejects `[port::u16 8080]`
#  is wrong.
#     ::T    explicit scalar type  (overrides §9 auto-typing)
#     ::T[]  explicit typed array  (overrides §9; items are T)
#     ::[]   inferred-type array   (element type inferred from items)

# ─── 8. Structural tokens & sigils ───────────────────────────────────────────────
[L60] Bracket ::= '[' | ']' | '{' | '}'                /* element/array | map */
[L61] Paren   ::= '(' | ')'                            /* SequenceLiteral [56a] */
[L64] Comma   ::= ','                                  /* item separator in [..] (..) {..} */
[L62] Sigil   ::= '$'      /* binding             */
                | '@'      /* attribute access    */
                | '='      /* attr pair / let-bind */
                | '/'      /* child path step      */
                | '.'      /* map-key path step    */
                | '*'      /* element wildcard     */
                | '**'     /* any-depth wildcard   */
                | '?'      /* error-propagate postfix (§9.2) */
                | '!'      /* panic postfix          (§9.2) */
[L63] DirectiveOpen ::= '[?' Ident                     /* `[?match` `[?def` … §4.1 */
#  The directive / clause-head / reserved-attr / reserved-bareword vocabulary
#  is the closed registry in code.md §4.1 — NOT duplicated here. The lexer
#  emits a directive-name token for any Ident after `[?`; the PARSER checks
#  membership and raises CXER0100 on a miss.
#  NOTE the `[?meta {…} FORM]` inert annotation directive (code.md §4.2) and
#  its reflection builtin `meta-of` (a reserved bareword head, no sigil) are
#  part of that registry — the annotation map rides with the value as an
#  inert side-band; only `meta-of` + the `<cx:meta>` serializer observe it.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  9. BODY VALUE CLASSIFICATION  (NORMATIVE — the "cx flavor" rule)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  THE decisive rule for "what does `[items …]` mean" (@CHOICE-1). One list
#  signal, by design:
#    • A COMMA (or a `::[]`/`::T[]` head, [L25d]) is the ARRAY signal — it makes a
#      single Array value. Whitespace NEVER auto-arrays.
#    • A no-comma body of SELF-DELIMITING tokens (typed scalars / atoms / quoted
#      strings — NO bareword) is a list of DISCRETE auto-typed children (mixed
#      content), NOT an array.
#    • A BAREWORD (an unquoted word / QName) in a no-comma body makes the whole
#      body ONE prose Text string (data-by-default conciseness).
#  This supersedes the old `[25a]` whitespace auto-array. The rule lives ONLY
#  here; both readers implement this one spec (so the historical AUTOARRAY/CONTENT
#  cross-parser drift cannot recur — it came from two copies, not from the rule).
#
#  Applies to an element body with NO explicit head TypeAnnotation ([L25d]
#  overrides). A child ELEMENT (or any non-leaf node) is ALWAYS a discrete child;
#  the rule below governs each contiguous run of LEAF tokens. Auto-type a token
#  via the SCALAR priority [L25a].
#
#  [L25a] ── ONE bare token, no comma → SCALAR, auto-typed (first match wins):
#       1. HexInt   2. Integer  → int        5. 'null'           → null
#       3. Float            → float           6. DateTimeLiteral  → datetime
#       4. 'true'|'false'   → bool            7. DateLiteral      → date
#       8. otherwise → Text (string)
#       `[port 8080]`→int   `[name Alice]`→string   `[d 2024-01-15]`→date
#
#  [L25b] ── TWO+ tokens, NO comma → DISCRETE ITEMS or PROSE (never an array).
#       A BAREWORD = an unquoted token that is NOT a typed-scalar literal, an
#       atom (`:name`), or a quoted string (a plain word or QName, e.g. `Alice`,
#       `a:b`). It is the prose signal:
#       • NO bareword (every token self-delimits — typed scalar / atom / quoted
#         string) → each token is its OWN child, auto-typed via [L25a]. The body
#         is MIXED CONTENT (discrete children), NOT an array and with NO element
#         data_type. NO promotion (int + float stay int, float).
#             `[ports 80 443]`              → int children 80, 443   (NOT int[])
#             `[xs 1 2.0 3]`                → children 1, 2.0, 3
#             `[flags true false]`          → bool children
#             `[ds 2024-01-15 2024-02-20]`  → date children
#             `[ks :on :off]`               → atom children :on, :off
#             `[mixed 1 "two" :three true]` → children 1, "two", :three, true
#             `[xs "a" "b"]`                → string children "a", "b"
#       • ANY bareword present → the WHOLE body is ONE verbatim PROSE Text string
#         (the run is not split; numbers / quoted tokens in it fold into the text).
#             `[title Getting Started]`     → text "Getting Started"
#             `[p the quick brown fox]`     → text "the quick brown fox"
#             `[p 404 not found]`           → text "404 not found"
#             `[x 1 a]`                     → text "1 a"
#       NOTES:
#       • `[p 1 2 3]` → THREE int children (no bareword), NOT the text "1 2 3"
#         and NOT int[]. Quote for text (`[p '1 2 3']`); comma for an array.
#       • Adjacent DISCRETE string children (`[xs "a" "b"]`) keep their boundary
#         only in LOSSLESS XML (`<cx:string>` carriers, conversions.md §0.2); the
#         idiomatic form collapses them (`<xs>ab</xs>`) and CX re-emits `[xs a b]`
#         which re-reads as the single prose string "a b". Prefer a comma array or
#         a single quoted run for a multi-string body that must round-trip.
#
#  [L25c] ── COMMA-SEPARATED items → a single ARRAY value (an ArrayNode; the
#       element holds one array). Each item auto-typed via [L25a]. The array is
#       HETEROGENEOUS — items keep their own types, with NO element `cx:type` and
#       NO numeric promotion. (A uniformly-typed `T[]` / inferred `[]` comes ONLY
#       from an explicit `::T[]` / `::[]` head, [L25d].)
#             `[tags web, prod, us-east]`  → Array ["web","prod","us-east"]  (no `::`)
#             `[ports 80, 443]`            → Array [80, 443]  (comma REQUIRED; without
#                                            it `[ports 80 443]` is discrete children, [L25b])
#             `[xs 1, 2.0]`                → Array [1, 2.0]   (no int→float promotion)
#             `[row 1, two, true]`         → Array [1, "two", true]  (mixed)
#
#  ── LITERAL COMMA IN PROSE → quote the run (single-quote canonical; the
#     renderer switches to double quotes only when the text contains a `'`):
#       `[p 'Hello, world']`          → text "Hello, world"   (one scalar)
#       `[p Hello, world]`            → Array ["Hello","world"] ← NOT prose! (comma)
#
#  ── EDGE CASES:
#       `[xs a,]`     → 1-item array ["a"]   (trailing comma forces a 1-array;
#                                             this is HOW you write a string singleton)
#       `[xs a]`      → scalar "a"            (no comma → scalar, not 1-array)
#       `[xs 5]`      → scalar int 5          (single token, no array)
#       `[xs]`        → empty element (no body value)
#       `[xs ,]` `[xs a,,b]` `[xs ,a]` → parse error (empty item; only a single
#                                        trailing comma is allowed, per [L84])
#       `[xs 'a, b', c]` → Array ["a, b", "c"]  (comma inside quotes literal)
#  ── To force a type the items don't auto-infer (e.g. sized) or to force a
#     STRING array from whitespace words, annotate the head:
#       `[xs::u16[] 1, 2, 3]`     `[xs::string[] alpha beta]` → string[]
#
#  [L25d] ── PRECEDENCE: an explicit head `::` TypeAnnotation ([L50]) OVERRIDES
#       this whole §9 classification (§9 runs ONLY when there is no annotation):
#       • `::T` (SCALAR type) → the body is ONE scalar of T; the entire body
#         text is coerced to T, with comma/whitespace becoming literal. `string`
#         always coerces (it is text); a non-string T that the body can't form
#         is a type error (cx-err:CXER0290 E_CAST_FAILED — the same code as a failed
#         `[cast …]`; covers a malformed value AND a sized iN/uN out-of-range).
#             `[items::string 1 2 3]`   → string "1 2 3"     (overrides int[])
#             `[items::string 1, 2, 3]` → string "1, 2, 3"   (commas literal)
#             `[n::int 1 2 3]`          → CXER0290 (body is not one int)
#       • `::T[]` (ARRAY type) → the body is an ARRAY of T; items split on
#         commas, or on whitespace when there are none; each coerced to T.
#             `[list::string[] a b c]`   → string[] ["a","b","c"]
#             `[list::string[] 'a b', c]`→ string[] ["a b","c"]
#       • `::[]` (inferred array) → array; element type inferred from items.
#       Reminder: the annotation glue is DOUBLE colon. `[items:string …]` is a
#       QName (element `string` in namespace `items`), NOT a type — §9 applies.

# ─── 10. Attribute values — ALWAYS A SINGLE SCALAR (no arrays, no nodes) ─────────
#  DECISION (D2): an attribute value is ALWAYS a single scalar.
#       AttValue ::= BareValue | QuotedText | TripleQuoted | RawTextStr  (scalar forms)
#       RawTextStr ::= '[#' RawContent '#]'   (the raw bytes AS A STRING scalar)
#  A bare attribute value is auto-typed by the [L25a] SCALAR priority
#  (`active=true`→bool, `nil=null`→null, `d=2024-01-15`→date; default string).
#  A `[# … #]` attribute value yields the raw content as a STRING scalar
#  (`a=[# x,y #]` → string "x,y") — it is NOT a RawText node; it is the
#  string-literal escape hatch for values that would otherwise need quoting.
#  Attributes NEVER hold a list or a node:
#    • the comma=list rule ([L25c]) does NOT apply to attributes — a comma is
#      an ordinary BareValue char, so `att=a,b` is the single string "a,b".
#    • a `[`/`{`/`(`-opened attribute value (Element / Array / Map / Sequence /
#      BlockContent) is a PARSE ERROR — only the `[#…#]` string form above is
#      admitted. Rich/list/nested data goes in a CHILD ELEMENT:
#      `[node [tags a, b]]` / `[a [meta [b …]]]`, not in an attribute.
#  THIS IS A DELIBERATE REMOVAL, not a contradiction-fix. The current core
#  spec SUPPORTS node-valued attributes — grammar.ebnf [55a] admits
#  `| RawText | BlockContent | CollectionLiteral | Element`; ast.md §Attribute
#  and conversions.md §2.1 (`<cx:attr cx:name=…>` child) specify their data
#  model + XML round-trip. D2 drops that feature: node-valued attributes only
#  round-trip to XML as non-idiomatic `<cx:attr>` CHILD elements (not real XML
#  attributes) and duplicate what child elements already express, while adding
#  undefined CXPath-into-attribute semantics. Scalar-only keeps attributes as
#  real XML attributes and CXPath `@name` as a plain scalar.
#  Graduation (DONE 2026-06-03) removed the node alternatives from grammar.ebnf
#  [55a]+[55c], the ast.md node-valued branch, the ast-bin.md node_flag wire
#  encoding, and the conversions.md `<cx:attr>` row; reconciled code.md §940
#  (non-scalar construction → CXER0100, not stringify).
[L70] BareValue ::= ( Char - (S | '[' | ']' | '=' | "'" | '"') )+   /* [12] */
#  BareValue stops at whitespace and  [ ] = ' "  — but allows / : # ? @ ! ~ +
#  (and ',') for URLs and paths. The comma is literal here (attributes only).

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  11. COLLECTION LITERALS & THE `[` DISAMBIGUATION  (sole home; from [56]-[56g])
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Three primitive collection forms, by bracket. Trailing comma always
#  allowed; canonical emit drops it (canonical.md §D14).
#
[L80] SequenceLiteral ::= '(' S? ItemList? S? ')'      /* [56a] */
#   FLATTENS nested sequences (CXDM §1 sequence-flat):
#       (1, (2, 3))  →  (1, 2, 3)        4-item example: ((a,b), c) → (a,b,c)
#       ()           →  empty Sequence
#   Parens have no other structural role, so there is no ambiguity.
#   SCOPE: flattening is a DATA-LAYER document convention. The PROGRAM layer
#   (cx code) treats a sequence as a FIRST-CLASS structured value and PRESERVES
#   nesting — `((a, b), c)` keeps rank, as multi-axis slices, `[?to-map]`
#   2-tuples, and nested-ring geometry require. (§9 [L25b] body classification
#   binds both layers; [L80] flattening does not — it is data-shape only.)
#
[L81] ArrayLiteral ::= '[' S? ItemList? S? ']'         /* [56b] */
#   NEST-PRESERVING (unlike Sequence):  [1, [2, 3]]  →  [1, [2, 3]]
#   Shares the `[` with Element [50] — disambiguated by [L83] below.
#
[L82] MapLiteral ::= '{' S? MapEntryList? S? '}'       /* [56c] */
#   {}  → empty Map.  Keys MUST be unique (dup key → W014 parse error).
#   Braces have no other structural role.
#
#  ── [L83] THE `[` DISAMBIGUATION (element vs array literal). This is the SOLE
#     canonical home; grammar [50.D] is a summary that MUST agree. Decided by the
#     FIRST token after `[`. Identical in BodyItem / Node position.
#       0. STRUCTURAL OPENERS bind FIRST (not subject to this rule): `[?`
#          directive, `[!` decl/doctype, `[#` raw text, `[-` comment, `[|` block,
#          `[$` call (program mode), `[*` alias, `[&` ref/anchor. CX has NO Markdown sigils —
#          `>`, `~`, `^`, the backtick, and `#` are ordinary content/operators,
#          never element-head openers.
#       1. `[]`  → empty Array.   (`[]` is never an element — an element needs
#                                  a Name head.)
#       2. first token is NOT a Name  → ARRAY LITERAL; items comma-separated
#          [L84]. (A scalar literal, a quoted/triple string, or a nested
#          `[`/`{`/`(`.)
#               [1, 2, 3]       → int array  [1, 2, 3]
#               ['a', 'b']      → string array ["a", "b"]
#               [[1,2], [3,4]]  → nested array (preserved, NOT flattened)
#               ['x y']         → 1-item array ["x y"]
#       3. first token IS a Name  → ELEMENT; that Name is the head, and the
#          body follows the BODY VALUE rule §9 (no comma → scalar/prose; comma
#          → typed array body):
#               [user x=1]          → element `user`, attribute x=1     (`=`)
#               [x y]               → element `x`, body scalar/prose `y`
#               [p the quick fox]   → element `p`, prose body
#               [ports 80, 443]     → element `ports`, comma body → int[]  ([L25c])
#               [tags web, prod]    → element `tags`, comma body → string[]
#               [xs a,]             → element `xs`, 1-item array ["a"]  (trailing,)
#
#     ⚠ A Name head IMMEDIATELY followed by `,` is a BARE BAREWORD ARRAY — it
#       is ambiguous with an element name and is a PARSE ERROR (CXER0100). To
#       get a bareword list, QUOTE the items or NAME the list:
#               [web, prod]   → ERROR — write ['web', 'prod']  or  [tags web, prod]
#               [web]         → element `web` (empty body), NOT the array ["web"]
#               [web,]        → ERROR (head `web`, empty body item)
#       Bare arrays of scalar literals / quoted strings are fine ([1,2], ['a','b']);
#       only an UNQUOTED leading bareword is reserved as an element head.
#
[L84] ItemList     ::= BodyItem (S? ',' S? BodyItem)* (S? ',')?   /* [56d] */
[L85] MapEntryList ::= MapEntry (S? ',' S? MapEntry)* (S? ',')?   /* [56e] */
[L86] MapEntry     ::= MapKey S? ':' S? BodyItem | ComputedEntry  /* [56f] */
[L87] MapKey       ::= Name | QuotedText | Scalar                 /* [56g] */
#   Bare-Name key == quoted-string key. `null` is NOT a valid key (→ W014).
#   ComputedEntry = `[?entry KEY-EXPR VAL]` (code.md §6.4.2), maps only.
#
#  ⚠ COLON ROLE #4.  The MapEntry `key : value` separator is the FOURTH use of a
#  lone `:` (atom prefix, type-guard head, namespace qualifier are the other
#  three per §2). It is unambiguous because it can only appear at that position
#  inside `{…}`. code.md §3.5 enumerates all four roles in agreement.
